[Music]  [Applause]  one of the most innovative companies in  fiber in the UK is city fiber and I'm  delighted to be joined today in Valencia  by Greg mesh the CEO Greg before we  start tell us about City Fiber and what  you've already achieved yeah well it's  been a really interesting and quite a  journey where seven years old now we're  publicly listed on the London Stock  Exchange so we're under the symbol City  we've started with a model where we did  acquisitions of duct infrastructure  across those secondary cities and then  we also built duct infrastructure under  contracts with the public sector so  we've done five acquisitions nine metro  builds that's got us across 42 cities  and we have roughly 3000 kilometers of  duct and fiber infrastructure in all of  those cities we've also done two  fiber-to-the-home builds one in  Bournemouth one under a joint venture  with Sky and talk talk where we proved  all the economics the one in York we  built out over the course of roughly a  year and a half it's 14,000 homes  currently over approaching 40%  penetration in that footprint so a huge  success or we proved all the economics  and and the the quality of services and  the uptake from the consumers and then  just recently we completed a what we  view is a transformational partnership  with Vodafone  where it is potentially to build up to  20% of the UK 5 million homes and to  start with 12 cities and a million homes  and were right there at the start of  that tell us a bit more about the  Vodafone because as you say that is  really transformational yes it is in a  lot of people say that must take me a  year to get done it actually took us  seven years because we had to prove  ourselves we had to prove our  engineering skills we had to prove our  technology we had to prove that we could  do it and I think that's a huge credit  to city Fiber to be able to have  positioned ourselves in that position  but because we sit with core duct  infrastructure across 42 cities that's  already equaling roughly about 4 million  homes than what we've proven is how we  can take that core infrastructure and  rapidly roll out a fibre to the home  network or fibre to the premises network  off that Vodafone's contract with us did  two things they said number one we can't  conceive of a world where the UK has  only one fibre provider as in open reach  so we need to diversify that so having  multiple suppliers of fibre was going to  be good for everybody so the framework  is for up to 20% of the UK and to start  with no less than a million homes across  roughly 12 cities the other thing that's  transformational about that is that  because of our model and we have no  legacy infrastructure legacy assets we  can do it fast and we can do it without  any burdensome overheads or any any fear  of the copper revenue migrations and so  we can actually give a better economic  value to Vodafone so one they get a  diversify the whole supply chain in the  UK but they also get better economics to  serve their customers is there anything  that the Europe can learn from this  model well I think one of the greatest  things that we're all learning now is  that like city fiber in the UK was a  spark for the industry we have been  consistent for seven years about doing  this and you could say that the entire  move that BT open reach is beginning to  make and the the great announcement  TalkTalk made last week that they want  to do this all of those are driven off  of a spark and we are that spark that  helped to drive that but I think the  real message is and that it seems to be  lost on a lot but because we have no  legacy infrastructure we have no legacy  systems we're not trapped behind the  copper revenue dilemma we can quickly  roll out cities fall over build cities  and we can do it at a better economic  value than anybody else so the alternate  operators are really where the  government's and the regulators should  focus because if they focus their  attention on helping somebody  demonstrate 10 20 30 percent of their  market more efficiently than it will  drive the incumbents to build more  efficiently supply better quality  services at lower costs for everybody in  the market so I really think the lesson  is is that it requires sparks in each of  these countries and those sparks are  usually their alternate operators Gregg  thank you very much for talking to us  thank you  so City Fiber a spark for Europe  [Music]  you

